title: When the JSON Does Not Fit
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

The cost of hiding the profile is that a mismatch would otherwise be a
dead end: the recipient has no editor, no path picker, no way to see
what the importer expected.  So the importer says it in words.  Try
these in the ''Import Reading List'' importer that ships with this wiki.

''The records are somewhere else.''  Paste `{"books": [{"title": "Dune"}]}`
-- the same records, wrapped in an object -- and click ''Stage books'':

> ''Nothing was staged.''  The records path `{{[*]}}` matched nothing: the document is an object with keys books, not an array.

''The fields are different.''  Paste `[{"name": "Dune", "by": "Herbert", "year": "1965"}]`:

> ''This JSON doesn't look like what this importer expects.''  It was built for records with the fields title, author, year, genre, rating, notes; the records here have name, by, year.<br>Missing here: title, author, genre, rating, notes<br>Different: year was number, here it is string<br>Present here but unused by the importer: name, by

That comparison is possible because the pack recorded the shape of its
sample when it was generated.  An importer generated without a sample
still gets the other messages, but not this one.

''Every title came out empty.''  When the title binding finds nothing
in any record, the panel says how many records that affected, which
binding produced the title, and what the first such record looked like
-- the keys it had, and an excerpt.

[img[Mismatched JSON.png]]

Each of these ends with the same sentence:

> If your JSON is what it should be and this keeps happening, the importer itself needs updating -- its profile was written for a different shape.  Ask Scott Sauyet, who made this importer (studio); there is nothing to fix on your side.

The name is the importer's author, as entered in the generator, and
"studio" links to the wiki it was generated in.  The recipient's job is
to forward the JSON; the author's job is the next section.
